\/Cycles\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/cycleBasis()","type":"topic","title":"cycleBasis()","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A cycle basis: the fundamental cycles of the breadth-first spanning forest, one for each"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"edge outside the forest, in ascending position of that edge, so "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edgeCount - vertexCount + c"},{"type":"text","text":" cycles for c components. Roots are taken in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"type":"text","text":" order and edges in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidentEdges"},{"type":"text","text":" order; a vertex’s tree edge is the one that discovered it. Self-loops and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"parallel edges are supported: a loop is a cycle of its own, and a second copy of an edge"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"closes a cycle of length 2."}],"fragments":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"cycleBasis","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> [","kind":"text"},{"text":"Cycle","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:5Walks5CycleV"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"text":"Vertex","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P6VertexQa"},{"text":", ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"text":"Edges","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P5EdgesQa"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"text":"Index","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:Sl5IndexQa"},{"text":">]","kind":"text"}]},"doc://Cycles/documentation/Cycles/GraphProtocols/Graph/isAcyclic":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Cycles\/documentation\/Cycles\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/isAcyclic","type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Whether the graph has no cycle (is a forest). A self-loop is a cycle, and so is a pair of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"t